  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nWell, today's convection has lingered longer over more of the\nQuad State than expected, and the complete lack of warming\ncertainly is concerning for the possibility of any significant\nsevere storms this evening or overnight. There is still a strong\nnorth or northeasterly push at the surface to the south over \nsouthern Tennessee and eastern Arkansas, so the better airmass \nwill not have much time to get back north over the Quad State. \nTherefore, the CAPE forecasts for this evening are likely \noverdone. The shear will be there, but if we struggle to get \nsurface-based convection, much of it may not be realized. \n\nThe CAMs have not had a clue about today's convection, so it\nwould be dangerous to accept their solutions for tonight.\nHowever, they have been consistently hammering on the 6-8 PM \nperiod for storms to reach far northwestern portions of the Quad\nState, with the northern extent over Perry and Jackson Counties\nin Illinois. Storms would then move east southeast across the \nregion generally south of a Duquoin Illinois to Greenville \nKentucky line. Northeast of this line storms cannot be ruled \nout, but given that that area will be farther from the better \nsurface air mass, severe storms are much less likely. There is \nsome sign of storms developing along I-64 into the overnight \nhours. Those storms would move east southeast and dissipate by \nsunrise over the Tri State.\n\nIf severe storms develop it would most likely be over Southeast\nMissouri where air mass recovery would presumably be most\nlikely. The CAPE and shear space advertised by the CAMs would\nsupport all modes of severe, but there has been a strong\ntendency for bow echoes in the models with a more significant \ndamaging wind threat. If a west northwest to east southeast \nboundary develops on the northeast edge of the better airmass, a\ngreater tornado threat could develop along it. Once again this \nappears to be most likely over southeast Missouri, possibly into\nthe Purchase Area of west Kentucky. \n\nThe timing for tonight could be delayed a few hours which would\nallow more time for air mass recovery, so it will not be\nsurprising if storms don't reach the area until 9 PM or later\nand then possibly last til around 2 or 3 AM before exiting west\nKentucky depending on how far northeast the better air mass\nmakes it.\n\nThe storms tonight should move along at a good clip, but we will\nhave to watch for swaths of torrential rains and possibly\nflooding impacts. This morning's storms produced a swath of 2-3\"\nover Sikeston Missouri and northeast into Wayne and Bollinger\ncounties. This area will be quite susceptible to flash flooding\nwith any storms tonight.\n\nThe convection should be done by daybreak Thursday, but an\nupper-level trough over the Quad State will keep a small chance\nof showers and storms going Thursday afternoon. Behind this\ntrough, we will remain under a somewhat busy cyclonic northwest\nflow aloft. This will bring the possibility of some convection\nattempting to dive southeast over the Quad State over the\nweekend and into the first half of next week. The models are not\nhandling individual disturbances very well, which results in the\nNBM spreading a lot of 15-30% PoPs across portions of the region\nfrom Friday night through next Wednesday. At least the northwest\nflow aloft will keep temperatures at or below normal for late\nAugust, which is quite welcome after last week's heat wave.",
